    {
      "id": "hIGxzY0-NoxqqVFv0m2u2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 379.482180380456,
      "y": 3217.9949887545426,
      "width": 1355.19970703125,
      "height": 1610,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bLS",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 828148643,
      "version": 394,
      "versionNonce": 833476899,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1730168821474,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "But the real question is how does the acedemind server know that we are refering to \nit when we type www.academid.com,i mean is www.academind.com is address of the server \njust like our house address-actually the answer is no:\n\n\nthe official addresss of any server is a IP address a internet protocol address\n\nAn IP address (Internet Protocol address) is a unique identifier assigned to each device\nconnected to a computer network that uses the Internet Protocol for communication. \nIt serves several key functions:\n\n\n1. Identification\nUnique Identifier: Each IP address is unique within a network, \nallowing devices to be distinctly identified. This uniqueness ensures that data packets \nare routed to the correct destination.\n\n2. Addressing\nLocation Address: An IP address specifies the location of a device on a network. \nIt acts like a mailing address, enabling the transmission of data between devices.\n\n3. Communication\nData Exchange: IP addresses are essential for routing data packets between devices over \nthe internet. When you send or receive data, your device uses its IP address to ensure \nthe information reaches the correct recipient.\n\nTypes of IP Addresses\n1.IPv4 (Internet Protocol version 4):\n\n->Structure: A 32-bit address, typically represented as four decimal numbers separated \nby dots (e.g., 192.168.1.1).\n\n->Capacity: Allows for about 4.3 billion unique addresses. \nDue to the growth of the internet, IPv4 addresses are becoming scarce.\n\n\n2.IPv6 (Internet Protocol version 6):\n\n->Structure: A 128-bit address, usually represented as eight groups of four \nhexadecimal digits separated by colons (e.g., 2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334).\n\n->Capacity: Allows for a vastly larger number of unique addresses \n(approximately 340 undecillion addresses), addressing the limitations of IPv4.\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "But the real question is how does the acedemind server know that we are refering to \nit when we type www.academid.com,i mean is www.academind.com is address of the server \njust like our house address-actually the answer is no:\n\n\nthe official addresss of any server is a IP address a internet protocol address\n\nAn IP address (Internet Protocol address) is a unique identifier assigned to each device\nconnected to a computer network that uses the Internet Protocol for communication. \nIt serves several key functions:\n\n\n1. Identification\nUnique Identifier: Each IP address is unique within a network, \nallowing devices to be distinctly identified. This uniqueness ensures that data packets \nare routed to the correct destination.\n\n2. Addressing\nLocation Address: An IP address specifies the location of a device on a network. \nIt acts like a mailing address, enabling the transmission of data between devices.\n\n3. Communication\nData Exchange: IP addresses are essential for routing data packets between devices over \nthe internet. When you send or receive data, your device uses its IP address to ensure \nthe information reaches the correct recipient.\n\nTypes of IP Addresses\n1.IPv4 (Internet Protocol version 4):\n\n->Structure: A 32-bit address, typically represented as four decimal numbers separated \nby dots (e.g., 192.168.1.1).\n\n->Capacity: Allows for about 4.3 billion unique addresses. \nDue to the growth of the internet, IPv4 addresses are becoming scarce.\n\n\n2.IPv6 (Internet Protocol version 6):\n\n->Structure: A 128-bit address, usually represented as eight groups of four \nhexadecimal digits separated by colons (e.g., 2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334).\n\n->Capacity: Allows for a vastly larger number of unique addresses \n(approximately 340 undecillion addresses), addressing the limitations of IPv4.\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
