    {
      "id": "xTxRlMp-R0k3wNQCC07NM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 384.25498327947173,
      "y": 6673.553032129022,
      "width": 1294.199462890625,
      "height": 1645,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bLY",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1571712803,
      "version": 1893,
      "versionNonce": 1326177805,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1730169621408,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "So these Ip address are the actual address of the server and not www.academin.com\nbut we would not want to enter this long number into our url search engine\nwe could if we want to and we will see how we can but it so much hard to \nremember each site ip address so we came up a system to access website\n\nand it's very much similar to how we declare a variable :\n->so when we declare a variable in order to store a value the whole \npoint is we give memory a named refrence to store a data\n\nso when we say:\n\nint num1=3;\n\nnum1---points to a memory address in the memory allocation(RAM) and since \nthese memory address are hard to access as they are hexadecimal address \nhard to access and hard to remember \n\nwe use variable which creates a refrence for that memory address and we\ncan access that memory address using variable \n\nsimilar to variable and memory address we came up with solution \nof DNS - Domain naming system in which Each ip address and port number\nwill be mapped to a unique domain id :so here www.academind.com is the domain\nname mapped to IP address \n\n\nNow just like how we can access memory address using variable through pointer\n\nlike :\n\nint main()\n{\n        int num1=9;\n        int *ptr=&num1;//here this ptr will store num1 address\n        cout<<ptr<<end;//this will print the memory address of num1 onto the\n//screen\n}\n\nOutpur:\n0xfff890134xf//a memory hexadecimal address\n\n\nSo just like how we can access memory address of variable using pointer \nand refrence we can access ip address map to the domain name using ping command :\n\n\n ",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "So these Ip address are the actual address of the server and not www.academin.com\nbut we would not want to enter this long number into our url search engine\nwe could if we want to and we will see how we can but it so much hard to \nremember each site ip address so we came up a system to access website\n\nand it's very much similar to how we declare a variable :\n->so when we declare a variable in order to store a value the whole \npoint is we give memory a named refrence to store a data\n\nso when we say:\n\nint num1=3;\n\nnum1---points to a memory address in the memory allocation(RAM) and since \nthese memory address are hard to access as they are hexadecimal address \nhard to access and hard to remember \n\nwe use variable which creates a refrence for that memory address and we\ncan access that memory address using variable \n\nsimilar to variable and memory address we came up with solution \nof DNS - Domain naming system in which Each ip address and port number\nwill be mapped to a unique domain id :so here www.academind.com is the domain\nname mapped to IP address \n\n\nNow just like how we can access memory address using variable through pointer\n\nlike :\n\nint main()\n{\n        int num1=9;\n        int *ptr=&num1;//here this ptr will store num1 address\n        cout<<ptr<<end;//this will print the memory address of num1 onto the\n//screen\n}\n\nOutpur:\n0xfff890134xf//a memory hexadecimal address\n\n\nSo just like how we can access memory address of variable using pointer \nand refrence we can access ip address map to the domain name using ping command :\n\n\n ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
