      "updated": 1730175382018,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1.Prototcol :so basically when client sends a request it need to specify \nwhich protocol it is following to communicate with backend basically so 95 \npercent of the time client follows either htttp or https where http stands for \nhyper text transfer protocol and https stands for hyper text transfer protocol secure .\n\n2.Address/port : Specifies the location of the file or resource within the website's \ndirectory structure that basically where in the backend the website is hosted so when \ntesting a website on your local network we see inside the search bar of browser one thing that \nis :localhost:3000 which basically means localhost->points to our local machine acting as server \nand 3000->refers to the port number where files are being served sometime localhost is also \nrepresented by the machine ip address :localhost(127.0.0.1) and then we can add port number \nto access website: http://127.0.0.1:3000/\n\nLets see this in action we wont be building a server but we will use vscode live server extension\nto create a dummy server now this does not provide us a http server but for understanding purpose it's\ngood to see so,\n\ngo to vscode -download a live server extension just to test out our html page \non live server basically it will create a backend channel to host html page on our machine\n",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1.Prototcol :so basically when client sends a request it need to specify \nwhich protocol it is following to communicate with backend basically so 95 \npercent of the time client follows either htttp or https where http stands for \nhyper text transfer protocol and https stands for hyper text transfer protocol secure .\n\n2.Address/port : Specifies the location of the file or resource within the website's \ndirectory structure that basically where in the backend the website is hosted so when \ntesting a website on your local network we see inside the search bar of browser one thing that \nis :localhost:3000 which basically means localhost->points to our local machine acting as server \nand 3000->refers to the port number where files are being served sometime localhost is also \nrepresented by the machine ip address :localhost(127.0.0.1) and then we can add port number \nto access website: http://127.0.0.1:3000/\n\nLets see this in action we wont be building a server but we will use vscode live server extension\nto create a dummy server now this does not provide us a http server but for understanding purpose it's\ngood to see so,\n\ngo to vscode -download a live server extension just to test out our html page \non live server basically it will create a backend channel to host html page on our machine\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
