    {
      "id": "8QYT3e9IbPAgHDNhyWrho",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 668.0952922979328,
      "y": 74816.243189484,
      "width": 2177.998779296875,
      "height": 945,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bOi",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 947478221,
      "version": 12,
      "versionNonce": 479606883,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1730179019409,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n4.Header body and query params\n-Now what are header body and query params -basically header body are the meta data our client sends to \nsever in order to retrive that particular data for particular set of users \n\nQuery parameters are a way to pass information to an API in a flexible and simple manner. \nThey are added to the end of the API endpoint URL as a series of key-value pairs. For example, \nconsider the following API endpoint:\n\nhttps://www.example.com/api/items?sort=asc&category=books\n\nIn this example, ‘sort’ and ‘category’ are query parameters that are \npassed to the API to specify the desired sorting order and category of items to retrieve.\n\nHeaders, on the other hand, provide additional information about the API request and response. \nThey are used to carry information such as the request method (GET, POST, etc.), the content type of the \nrequest payload, authentication information, and other metadata. Headers are included in the HTTP request and \nresponse messages, separate from the actual request and response payloads.\n\nFor example, consider the following HTTP request header\n",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n4.Header body and query params\n-Now what are header body and query params -basically header body are the meta data our client sends to \nsever in order to retrive that particular data for particular set of users \n\nQuery parameters are a way to pass information to an API in a flexible and simple manner. \nThey are added to the end of the API endpoint URL as a series of key-value pairs. For example, \nconsider the following API endpoint:\n\nhttps://www.example.com/api/items?sort=asc&category=books\n\nIn this example, ‘sort’ and ‘category’ are query parameters that are \npassed to the API to specify the desired sorting order and category of items to retrieve.\n\nHeaders, on the other hand, provide additional information about the API request and response. \nThey are used to carry information such as the request method (GET, POST, etc.), the content type of the \nrequest payload, authentication information, and other metadata. Headers are included in the HTTP request and \nresponse messages, separate from the actual request and response payloads.\n\nFor example, consider the following HTTP request header\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "smrO0LvEWwwyQVZ0TlLSt",
      "type": "image",
      "x": 757.5406312832712,
      "y": 75741.00917939006,
      "width": 716.7418561573329,
      "height": 361.5283811894917,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "transparent",
