    {
      "id": "k_CszJlBcSCUxRiJO1-B6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 834.3167541004384,
      "y": 120952.13375469929,
      "width": 2177.99951171875,
      "height": 1260,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bQl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1915484963,
      "version": 31,
      "versionNonce": 894719949,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1730226822018,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Express.js is a minimal and flexible web application framework for Node.js \nthat provides a robust set of features for building web and mobile applications. \nIt simplifies the process of creating server-side applications by providing a range of tools \nand middleware for handling HTTP requests, routing, and managing server-side logic.\n\nKey Features of Express.js\n1.Minimalist Framework: Express provides a thin layer of fundamental web application features, \nallowing developers to build web applications with minimal boilerplate code.\n\n2.Middleware Support: Express allows the use of middleware functions, which are functions that have \naccess to the request and response objects. Middleware can modify the request and response, \nend the request-response cycle, or call the next middleware in the stack.\n\n3.Routing: Express provides a powerful routing mechanism that allows you to define routes for \nhandling different HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.) and URL paths. \nThis makes it easy to create RESTful APIs.\n\n4.Template Engine Support: You can integrate various template engines (like Pug, EJS, Handlebars) \nto generate dynamic HTML pages.\n\n5.Static File Serving: Express can serve static files (like HTML, CSS, JS) from a designated directory with a \nsimple setup.\n\n6.Error Handling: Built-in mechanisms for error handling help manage errors effectively and provide \nmeaningful feedback to clients.\n\n8.Extensibility: You can easily integrate third-party middleware and libraries to extend the functionality \nof your Express application.",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Express.js is a minimal and flexible web application framework for Node.js \nthat provides a robust set of features for building web and mobile applications. \nIt simplifies the process of creating server-side applications by providing a range of tools \nand middleware for handling HTTP requests, routing, and managing server-side logic.\n\nKey Features of Express.js\n1.Minimalist Framework: Express provides a thin layer of fundamental web application features, \nallowing developers to build web applications with minimal boilerplate code.\n\n2.Middleware Support: Express allows the use of middleware functions, which are functions that have \naccess to the request and response objects. Middleware can modify the request and response, \nend the request-response cycle, or call the next middleware in the stack.\n\n3.Routing: Express provides a powerful routing mechanism that allows you to define routes for \nhandling different HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.) and URL paths. \nThis makes it easy to create RESTful APIs.\n\n4.Template Engine Support: You can integrate various template engines (like Pug, EJS, Handlebars) \nto generate dynamic HTML pages.\n\n5.Static File Serving: Express can serve static files (like HTML, CSS, JS) from a designated directory with a \nsimple setup.\n\n6.Error Handling: Built-in mechanisms for error handling help manage errors effectively and provide \nmeaningful feedback to clients.\n\n8.Extensibility: You can easily integrate third-party middleware and libraries to extend the functionality \nof your Express application.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
